{%- comment -%}
  Fabric & care, on a product page.

  Asked for 2026-09-27: "tambahin detail fabric per product... di fabric and care
  nya jelasin linen". The material was already on the page — `product-card` and
  the buy box print it as a one-word line above the title — but one word above
  the title is a label, not an explanation, and the section actually headed
  Fabric & care was rendering a grey placeholder on all seventeen products.

  KEYED BY MATERIAL, NOT WRITTEN PER PRODUCT, and that is the whole design. The
  catalogue is seventeen products across EIGHT fabrics: three cardigans share
  one knit, two culottes share one linen, five trousers share one semiwool. A
  per-product field would be the same paragraph typed three times and three
  places to correct when a care line turns out to be wrong. This is the same
  argument `size-guide.liquid` makes for the size chart being store-wide, and it
  is made here for the same reason.

  A NEW PRODUCT INHERITS ITS FABRIC'S COPY FOR FREE. That is the real payoff:
  the studio adds Tara Stripe Pants with `custom.material = semiwool stripe` and
  the page is complete without anyone writing a word. It is also the risk — a
  material spelled a new way matches nothing and draws the placeholder, which is
  correct behaviour and will look like a bug. It is not: silently showing the
  nearest other fabric's care instructions would be the bug.

  PRECEDENCE, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE SIZE CHART'S AND THE SHOPEE
  LINK'S: the specific beats the general. A product carrying its own
  `custom.care` metafield renders that and nothing else — for a piece with a
  lining, a trim or a finish the fabric alone does not describe. The glossary is
  the fallback, and a labelled placeholder is the fallback to that.

  THE CARE LINE IS THE STUDIO'S TO CONFIRM. The descriptions say what each cloth
  is, which is drawn from the material names the studio themselves supplied; the
  care sentences are conservative for the fibre — cold, shade, low iron — rather
  than measured off a care label nobody has sent. Wrong care copy ruins a
  garment, so this is flagged in HANDOVER.md as something to confirm before
  launch rather than treated as settled.

  Accepts: product
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign own_care = product.metafields.custom.care.value
  assign material = product.metafields.custom.material.value | strip | downcase

  assign fabric_description = ''
  assign fabric_care = ''

  if own_care == blank and material != blank
    assign glossary = settings.fabric_care | strip | newline_to_br
    assign glossary = glossary | replace: '<br />', '|~|' | replace: '<br>', '|~|'
    assign entries = glossary | split: '|~|'

    for entry in entries
      assign parts = entry | split: '|'
      assign key = parts[0] | strip | downcase
      if key == material
        assign fabric_description = parts[1] | strip
        assign fabric_care = parts[2] | strip
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}

{%- if own_care != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The per-product metafield is rich text, so it arrives as markup and is
    printed rather than wrapped in a paragraph of our own — Shopify already
    emitted one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {{ own_care }}

{%- elsif fabric_description != blank -%}
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
    <p>{{ fabric_description }}</p>
    {%- if fabric_care != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The care sentence is set one step down rather than run into the
        paragraph above it. They answer different questions — what is this, and
        what do I do with it — and a shopper checking the second one is
        scanning, not reading.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="text-caption leading-snug text-ink-muted">{{ fabric_care }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No metafield and no glossary line for this material — the labelled
    placeholder at final size, like every other empty slot on this site.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'copy', value: blank, label: 'fabric and care', lines: 3 %}
{%- endif -%}
